preConditionHook:
// PERMISSION TO REVERSE OUT OF A COUNTERTREND POSITION. Brooks, R28-45: a
// countertrend trade that is met by a SECOND with-trend counting setup should
// be exited or reversed -- a short held into a high 2 in a bull trend, a long
// held into a low 2 in a bear trend. This is an ENTRY that reads position
// state deliberately, the same shape as FlatOrOpenLossExceeds and
// EntryBarClosedAgainstPosition: it decides whether a fresh with-trend signal
// may reverse the open trade, and the engine's own reversal behaviour turns
// the entry into the exit.
//
// LONG fires while SHORT: the high count has just advanced to countTarget, so
// the with-trend bull signal has arrived against the countertrend short.
// SHORT is the mirror: long held, low count just advanced.
//
// THE 'BULL TREND' HALF OF THE MERGED PSEUDOCODE IS NOT CARRIED. The entry
// pane ANDs its rules, so trend context belongs to a separate rule placed
// beside this one; folding it in would make this rule unparameterizable and
// is exactly the compound the authoring skill splits. The MA-proximity
// emphasis in the book is a strengthening condition, not a requirement, and
// is likewise left to a placed EMA rule.
//
// NOT OpposingCountExit. That rule is an EXIT reading the opposite counter as
// a standing level; this is an ENTRY, and it carries the ADVANCE FLAG, so it
// fires only on the signal bar itself -- the bar whose close is the corpus's
// signal-bar-close / next-bar-open trigger. Slice B06 ruled on the pair
// explicitly when it authored the counter.
//
// THE COUNTER IS THE SETTLED ONE (Ranges ch17, R17-01), inlined here as
// everywhere -- see HighLowBarCount for the full commentary. A bar whose high
// exceeds the prior bar's high advances the count, but only once a new low
// has formed since the last counted bar; the count resets on a fresh
// resetLookback-bar high of the close and saturates at 4. The reset window is
// Highest(high, resetLookback)[1], the N bars ENDING ONE BAR AGO, so the
// current bar's own high cannot defeat its own reset test. Highest/Lowest
// bottom out in EL's Extremes(), which is EasyLanguage SOURCE and so carries
// the relational tolerance -- hence el_gt / el_lt and not raw > and <.
//
// ctx.MarketPosition() is the measured accessor (+1 / 0 / -1), read in the
// condition exactly as EasyLanguage's MarketPosition is.
//
// WARM-UP: minLowSince and signalLow start at 0 like every EL variable, so on
// positive prices the first advance can only come through the highCount == 0
// branch. The EL twin's variables start at the same 0.
priorHigh = high[1];
priorLow = low[1];
for (int i = 2; i <= (int)resetLookback; ++i) {
    if (el_gt(high[i], priorHigh)) priorHigh = high[i];
    if (el_lt(low[i], priorLow)) priorLow = low[i];
}
advHigh = false;
advLow = false;
if (el_gt(close[0], priorHigh)) {
    highCount = 0;
} else {
    if (el_lt(low[0], minLowSince)) minLowSince = low[0];
    if (el_gt(high[0], high[1]) && (highCount == 0 || el_lt(minLowSince, signalLow))) {
        highCount = highCount + 1;
        if (highCount > 4) highCount = 4;
        signalLow = low[0];
        minLowSince = low[0];
        advHigh = true;
    }
}
if (el_lt(close[0], priorLow)) {
    lowCount = 0;
} else {
    if (el_gt(high[0], maxHighSince)) maxHighSince = high[0];
    if (el_lt(low[0], low[1]) && (lowCount == 0 || el_gt(maxHighSince, signalHigh))) {
        lowCount = lowCount + 1;
        if (lowCount > 4) lowCount = 4;
        signalHigh = high[0];
        maxHighSince = high[0];
        advLow = true;
    }
}
